<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Guria</h1><p class="st-meta">1460 CE – 1829 CE (approx.) · capital Ozurgeti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small principality on Georgia's Black Sea coast, ruled by the Gurieli family between the Kingdom of Imereti and the Ottoman Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Guria, the land south of the Rioni along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, was governed by a branch of the Vardanidze family, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gurieli">Gurieli</a>, as eristavis of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a>. As the kingdom broke apart in the 1460s, the Gurieli became effectively independent princes, nominally owing allegiance to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The princes ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozurgeti">Ozurgeti</a> over a land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, vineyards and forested hills. Guria lay on the frontier with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which seized its coastal fortresses and drew tribute; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> through Ottoman ports was a persistent evil. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gurieli">Gurieli</a> took part in Imereti's dynastic wars, sometimes ruling it themselves, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamia-iii-gurieli">Mamia III</a> did.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1810 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamia-v-gurieli">Mamia V</a> accepted a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. After his death in 1826 his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> succeeded under the regency of his mother Sophia; a revolt in 1828 was crushed, and in 1829 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed Guria.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-guria.jpg" alt="Principality of Guria" width="320" height="361"><figcaption>Image: ArnoldPlaton · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_Guria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1460 CE – 1829 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ozurgeti</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mamia I Gurieli, Kakhaber Gurieli, Mamia V Gurieli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Georgia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guria, Gurieli principality</dd></dl></aside></div>
